{% set local_problem %}
<p>I'll tell you exactly what lake humidity does to fascia boards.</p>

<p>We pulled gutters off a 14-year-old house at <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> last summer for a replacement job. The homeowner thought his fascia was fine — paint looked decent, no obvious sagging. But when we got the old gutters down, three sections of fascia had rot damage we could see, and two more had soft spots you could only find by pressing on them.</p>

<p>Here's how it happens: The gutter develops a small leak at a seam or corner. Not enough to drip visibly — just a slow seep that wicks into the fascia behind. On a dry day in Huntsville, that fascia would dry out by afternoon. Near Lake Guntersville, where humidity runs 75-85% most summer days, it never fully dries. The wood stays damp. Fungi move in. Rot starts.</p>

<p>This process accelerates on vacation homes. A small leak develops after you leave. Nobody notices it. Moisture accumulates for months. By your next visit, what started as a pinhole seam failure has caused $500-1,500 worth of wood damage.</p>

<p>The fix is straightforward but important: replace damaged wood with properly primed lumber or composite materials, seal all edges against moisture, and install gutters that don't leak. Installing new gutters on rotted fascia is pointless — the screws pull out, the gutters sag, and you've wasted your money.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Here's how we approach fascia repair:</p>

<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> We check every inch of fascia on every gutter job — not just the obvious problem areas. We press on boards to find soft spots. We look for paint bubbles (moisture underneath). We check where gutters meet fascia for seepage stains. Rot often hides behind what looks like intact wood.</p>

<p><strong>Removal:</strong> Old gutters come down first. Then damaged fascia sections get cut out — we go past the rot into solid wood on both ends. Cutting into apparently good wood sometimes reveals more rot underneath. If that happens, we keep going until we hit solid material.</p>

<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> Once fascia is removed, we inspect what's behind it. Usually it's fine — rot rarely spreads to rafters unless it's been there for years. But if we find structural damage (soft rafter tails, compromised sheathing), we stop and discuss options before proceeding. You need to know what you're dealing with.</p>

<p><strong>Replacement:</strong> New fascia goes in — either primed lumber (1x6 or 1x8 depending on your trim profile) or composite material. Composite costs more but handles humidity better and won't rot. We prime all cuts and edges before installation. Joints get caulked. Everything gets painted or matched to your existing trim.</p>

<p><strong>Gutter installation:</strong> New gutters go on fresh, solid fascia. Hangers get screwed into good wood every 18-24 inches. The whole system starts from a solid foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

